From f89d509e95bd40231a766cb9fdcaea0dd0ac67f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 20:56:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: samsung: gtaxl: reference codec PLL1 to the AMP-port
 BCLK

The RT5659 is not on the AP-side bus.  It hangs off the s1402x mixer's
AMP port, which the mixer probe fixes at 64fs / 24 bit (INAMP_CTL =
0x2a) and never reprograms at runtime, neither in this driver nor in
the vendor one.  The BCLK the codec sees is therefore always 64fs,
while bfs describes only the AP -> mixer input, which is 32fs at
48 kHz.

Feeding the AP-side figure to snd_soc_dai_set_pll() understated the
PLL reference by two at 48 kHz.  rl6231_pll_calc() picked N=46, K=1
with M bypassed, a x16 ratio, for what it believed was a 1.536 MHz
reference; against the real 3.072 MHz that leaves PLL1 running at
49.152 MHz, i.e. 1024fs.  set_sysclk() right below then told the codec
SYSCLK was 24.576 MHz, so rt5659_hw_params() programmed the converter
dividers in ADDA_CLK_1 for 512fs and every internal filter and
sigma-delta clock ran at twice its intended rate.

Playback stayed at the right pitch, since data is consumed at LRCK,
and a digital loopback through DAC MIX stayed transparent, since that
path never leaves the fs domain.  The audible symptom was broadband
high-frequency noise over the whole output, on both the speaker and
the headphone.

Use the AMP-port ratio for the PLL reference.  At 192 kHz the two
ratios already coincided, so only the 48 kHz path changes.
---
 sound/soc/samsung/gtaxl_rt5659.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/samsung/gtaxl_rt5659.c b/sound/soc/samsung/gtaxl_rt5659.c
index aa8640df2f..9f7d03b2cb 100644
--- a/sound/soc/samsung/gtaxl_rt5659.c
+++ b/sound/soc/samsung/gtaxl_rt5659.c
@@ -35,6 +35,15 @@
 #define GTAXL_BFS_192KHZ	64
 #define GTAXL_PLL_192KHZ	49152000U
 
+/*
+ * The codec does not sit on the AP-side bus.  It hangs off the mixer's
+ * AMP port, which the s1402x probe fixes at 64fs / 24 bit
+ * (INAMP_CTL = 0x2a) and never reprograms at runtime, neither here nor
+ * in the vendor driver.  The BCLK arriving at the codec is therefore
+ * always 64fs, independent of the AP -> mixer ratio above.
+ */
+#define GTAXL_BFS_AMP		64
+
 static int gtaxl_aif1_hw_params(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 				struct snd_pcm_hw_params *params)
 {
@@ -91,8 +100,19 @@ static int gtaxl_aif1_hw_params(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	/* Codec: PLL1 from BCLK1, SYSCLK from PLL1 */
-	bclk = rate * bfs;
+	/*
+	 * Codec: PLL1 from BCLK1, SYSCLK from PLL1.
+	 *
+	 * The PLL reference is the BCLK the codec actually receives on
+	 * the mixer's AMP port, which is 64fs, not the AP-side bfs that
+	 * only describes the mixer input.  At 48 kHz the two differ by
+	 * two: declaring 32fs made rl6231_pll_calc() pick a x16 ratio
+	 * for a reference that is really 3.072 MHz, so PLL1 ran at
+	 * 49.152 MHz (1024fs) while set_sysclk() below told the codec
+	 * 24.576 MHz (512fs) and its converter dividers were programmed
+	 * from that figure.
+	 */
+	bclk = rate * GTAXL_BFS_AMP;
 	ret = snd_soc_dai_set_pll(codec_dai, 0, RT5659_PLL1_S_BCLK1,
 				  bclk, codec_pll);
 	if (ret < 0) {
-- 
